To apply for Supplemental Security Income (SSI) as an adult with a disability, you can file online if you meet specific requirements. According to the Social Security help pages:

You are eligible to file for SSI benefits online if you:
- Are between the ages of 18 and 65;
- Have never been married;
- Aren't blind;
- Are a U.S. citizen residing in one of the fifty states, District of Columbia, or the Northern Mariana Islands;
- Haven't applied for or received SSI benefits in the past; and
- Are applying for Social Security Disability Insurance at the same time as your SSI claim [p261].

If you meet these requirements, you can apply online for both SSI and Social Security Disability Benefits.

For an adult with a disability, you can apply for Supplemental Security Income (SSI) benefits. Here are the key details:

You can file for SSI online if you meet these requirements [p261]:
- Are between the ages of 18 and 65;
- Have never been married;
- Aren't blind;
- Are a U.S. citizen residing in one of the fifty states, District of Columbia, or the Northern Mariana Islands;
- Haven't applied for or received SSI benefits in the past; and
- Are applying for Social Security Disability Insurance at the same time as your SSI claim.

If you don't meet all these requirements, you may still be able to apply for SSI by visiting a local Social Security office or calling 1-800-772-1213.

The SSI program pays benefits to disabled adults and children who have limited income and resources [p258]. Whether you apply for Social Security disability or SSI, we ask you for information about your medical condition, work, and education history to help us decide if you are disabled under our rules [p231].

